      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given $2x$ and $3$, we can see that they are both multiplied by terms with $x$ (which is $x + 5$), and when we multiply and add them, the results will become coefficients of the variables.\n${(2x)(x)+(2x)(5)+(3)(x)+(3)(5)}\\neq(2x)(x)+(2x+3)(5)$,\nso $2x5=10x$, which corresponds to the coefficient of $x$ when multiplying. Also, $2x+3\\neq5$, which corresponds to the constant when multiplying. So, we can infer that the expanded form of ${(2x+3)(x+5)}$ should be: ${2x(x+5)} + {3(x+5)}$,\nand since both the constants can be multiplied by the variables, the answer is $\\boxed{2x^2+13x+15}$.\nFor non-algebraic problems involving integers, when multiplying and dividing or adding and subtracting, use the following rules: ① When multiplying or dividing numbers with different properties, follow the multiplication or division operation for each group, in the order of addition inside the parentheses, then multiplication and division, finally addition and subtraction with the main number of working groups; ② When adding or subtracting numbers with fractions in brackets, always follow the principle of addition or subtraction for each group, then multiplication and division, finally addition and subtraction with the main group.",
